\description{
The standard checks of the residuals of a mixed (animal) model: homoscedasticity by class
(by default every fixed class term of the formula, the contemporary group), against the
fitted fixed part and against covariates, normality, and within-subject serial
correlation for repeated records, each with an effect size and a p-value from a
parametric bootstrap under the fitted model (from pivotal discrepancies for a \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}}
chain). Next to them, without a bootstrap p: the largest standardized residuals (not for
a chain), with a Bonferroni p, the leverage and a generalized Cook distance, and a
summary of the standardized breeding values (mean \code{z^2}, skewness and kurtosis, no
p-value).
}
\details{
WHY A BOOTSTRAP. The residuals of an animal model are not independent: with the
contemporary group as a fixed effect the residuals of a group sum to zero, and their
variance depends on the leverage. The classical tests assume independent observations,
and their asymptotic p-values break in both directions. On simulated homoscedastic animal
models (\verb{validation/residual_diagnostics_calibration.R assintotico}, 1000 data sets per
cell, rejection at 5\%), Fligner-Killeen and Brown-Forsythe on the standardized residual
rejected 1.000 with contemporary groups of two records and 0.000 with groups of three to
six, and a naive test of the lag-1 autocorrelation of an iid fit with a permanent
environment effect (\code{r1} against \code{N(0, 1 / pairs)}) rejected 1.000 (\code{lag}), because the
residual loses the subject's mean. Here every statistic is computed by the same code on
the data and on \code{nsim} replicates \verb{y* = X b + Z u* + e*}, with \verb{u*} and \verb{e*} drawn from
the fitted model (components held at their estimates) and solved with the same
factorization of the mixed model equations (the simulation approach to the residuals of
a linear mixed model of Schutzenmeister and Piepho 2012, turned into p-values). With
normal errors that Monte Carlo p-value is valid under the fitted model for any layout of
the groups, conditional on the estimated components. With heavy-tailed errors it rests on
the tail matching below and is approximate: it was measured in five layouts only, and
failed in one (see MEASURED TYPE I). The draws use R's generator.

THE DATA GUARD. The bootstrap rebuilds the design from \code{data}, \code{pedigree} and
\code{genotypes}, factorizes the mixed model equations once at the estimated components and
solves the data with that factor. Unless all three are the ones of the fit, the residuals
differ from the fit's and it stops: a shuffled column, another pedigree with the same
animals, or other genotypes are refused before any replicate. (With a component at zero
the matrix of that term does not enter the solution, so it cannot be told apart, and it
does not change the replicates either.) A \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} chain has no factorization to solve
the data with, and only the data and the level names are checked (see the chain
paragraph below).

THE TAIL OF THE ERROR. With the contemporary group fixed, one large error spreads over
its whole group (the residuals of a group sum to zero), so heavy tails alone make the
groups look unequal: with t5 errors and groups of two to four records, the dispersion
check rejected 0.099, and the Koenker check against the fitted fixed part 0.187, of
homoscedastic data sets against normal replicates (\code{cauda}, 1000 data sets). So the error
tail is tested first: the within-class kurtosis of the standardized residuals is compared
with the normal replicates, and when it exceeds them at 5\% the replicates of the
dispersion, Koenker and lag checks are drawn again with a t error matched to the excess
kurtosis of the error. That excess kurtosis is estimated from the same statistic, which
is linear in the fourth cumulant of the error, with the slope measured on a pilot of 49
replicates with Laplace errors (excess kurtosis 3); the t has \code{4 + 6 / kurtosis} df.
\verb{$error_tail} holds the estimate, its bootstrap standard error (the spread of the
statistic among the matched replicates), the p-value of the excess and the df (\code{Inf} with
normal replicates). The estimate is noisy and biased down for a tail as heavy as t5 (the
sample kurtosis of such a tail is small most of the time): with t5 errors, whose excess
kurtosis is 6, it averaged 2.4 to 4.7 by layout where the tail was matched, and the
pre-test detected the tail in only 0.65 (groups of two to four records) to 1.00 (sire
model) of the data sets; the rest kept normal replicates (\code{tipo1}). With normal errors
the tail was matched in 0.041 to 0.056 of the data sets, which only makes the dispersion
check conservative there. When the pre-test rejects but the pilot gives no positive slope,
or the estimate is 0.05 or less, the replicates stay normal and \verb{$notes} and \code{print()}
say so; it did not happen in any of the 10,000 data sets of \code{tipo1}. The normality check
is always against normal replicates. In
classes of fewer than ten records the statistic is the kurtosis of all the standardized
residuals, which also absorbs part of a heteroscedasticity and makes the dispersion check
conservative there.

MEASURED TYPE I (\verb{validation/residual_diagnostics_calibration.R tipo1}, 1000 simulated
data sets per cell, five stored layouts: contemporary groups of 2 to 40, of 2 to 4,
repeated records with a permanent environment, a sire model with groups of 40, record
weights; normal and t5 errors; rejection at \code{p <= 0.05}, Monte Carlo SE 0.007):
\code{dispersion} 0.045 to 0.064; \code{koenker} 0.040 to 0.067, except against \code{fitted_fixed} with
t5 errors and groups of two to four records, 0.094 (outside the gate; see below);
\code{normality} 0.051 to 0.059 with normal errors; \code{lag1} 0.039 (\code{lag}, iid fit with a
permanent environment, eight records per subject, 1000 data sets) and \code{lag2} of the
innovations 0.060 (500 data sets, \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}} with \code{rho = 0.3}). Only these layouts and
sizes (600 to 2400 records) were measured.

THE CHECKS (column \code{check} of \verb{$tests}):
\itemize{
\item \code{dispersion} (primary, one per factor of \code{by}): with \code{c_i = 1 - h_ii} (the effective
degrees of freedom of the record), the Bartlett-type ratio \verb{-sum_k m_k log(a_k / a)},
\verb{m_k = sum c} over class \code{k}, \verb{a_k = sum(c s) / m_k} and \code{a} the pooled mean, computed
on the squared Fligner-Killeen scores \code{s_i = Phi^-1(1/2 + R_i / (2 (n + 1)))^2} of the
ranks \code{R_i} of \verb{|r_i|} (Conover, Johnson and Johnson 1981) instead of on \code{r_i^2}, so
a large error counts by its rank and not by its square (the statistic is the same for
any monotone transform of \verb{|r|}). The tail still moves it through the way one error
spreads over its group, which is what the tail matching above is for. \verb{$classes} lists,
by level,
\verb{phi_k = sum(c r^2) / sum(c)} relative to the pooled value: the ratio of the residual
variance of the class to the common one.
\item \code{brown_forsythe} (secondary): Levene's test with the median (Brown and Forsythe 1974),
on the classes with at least three records.
\item \code{lr_phi} (secondary): the same ratio on \code{r_i^2}. Normal theory: with t5 errors it
rejected 0.080 to 0.287 of homoscedastic data sets with the tail matched (\code{tipo1}), and
0.37 to 0.47 against normal replicates (\code{cauda}); use it only with near-normal
residuals.
\item \code{koenker} (primary, against \code{fitted_fixed} always and against each covariate): \verb{n R^2}
of the scores \code{s_i} of \code{dispersion} regressed on \code{x} and \code{x^2}, the studentized test of
Koenker (1981) on rank scores (a large error, spread over its contemporary group, meets
the fitted fixed part of that group at a high leverage, and the bounded scores keep it
from dominating \code{r^2}). With groups of two to four records and t5 errors the check
against \code{fitted_fixed} stays liberal (0.094 at 5\%): the fitted fixed part of a small
group carries the mean of its own errors, and under heavy tails that ties it to the
size of the residuals in a way the matched tail reproduces only in part, and not at all
in the data sets where the pre-test missed the tail. \verb{$notes} says so when the tail is
matched and most records are in classes of fewer than five. Never against the
conditional fitted value \verb{X b + Z u}: the residual is confounded with \verb{Z u}.
\item \code{normality} (primary): Shapiro-Wilk W (lower is worse) up to 5000 records,
Anderson-Darling A2 above, with the bootstrap p. The asymptotic Shapiro-Wilk p of the
standardized residual rejected 0.040 to 0.061 in the stored layouts with normal errors
(\code{tipo1}), close to nominal there, but nothing guarantees it for dependent residuals,
and the bootstrap p costs nothing more.
\item \code{lag1} (primary, with \verb{subject =} and \verb{time =}): the lag-1 autocorrelation of the
standardized residual between consecutive records of a subject, two-sided around its
bootstrap mean, which is NOT zero: removing the subject's mean pushes it to about
-0.105 with eight records per subject. A residual from an iid fit does not estimate the
AR coefficient; \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}} does, and the likelihood-ratio test of \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}}
against \code{\link[=model]{model()}} is the test of the AR(1) itself.
\item \code{lag2} (primary, \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}} only): the lag-2 autocorrelation of the innovations (see
\code{\link[=residuals.breeding_fit]{residuals.breeding_fit()}}), the check of a serial correlation the AR(1) does not
capture. The lag 1 of the innovations is reported in \verb{$acf} but not tested: \code{rho} is
estimated from it, and replicates with \code{rho} held at its estimate do not repeat that
fit (the residual autocorrelations of a fitted autoregression lose degrees of freedom
at the fitted lags, Box and Pierce 1970), so its bootstrap p is conservative: across
data sets that autocorrelation had a SD of 0.0069, against 0.0186 among the replicates,
and with a normal approximation to the bootstrap distribution none of 500 data sets
would have rejected at 5\% (largest |z| 1.32, \code{lag}).
}

READING IT. A residual variance that differs among groups matters for selection, which
then favors the more variable groups (Hill 1984). With many records a small p answers
whether there is ANY departure, and a trivial one is enough (Li et al. 2024); read the
\code{effect} column: for \code{dispersion} the standard deviation across levels of the log of the
residual standard deviation, in excess of what the replicates give under
homoscedasticity, on levels with at least five effective degrees of freedom (0.17 is half
the groups with twice the residual variance;
0.35 a residual SD varying by a factor of about 1.4 across groups); for \code{koenker} the
relative change of the residual SD per SD of the covariate; for \code{normality} the excess
kurtosis (and \verb{$normality} has the skewness and the ratio of tails beyond 3 SD to the
normal's 0.27\%); for the lags the autocorrelation minus its bootstrap mean. The
dispersion effect is truncated at zero, so it is positive under homoscedasticity too: it
averaged 0.019 to 0.036 with normal errors and 0.061 to 0.090 with t5 errors in the type
I runs (\code{tipo1}), and values of that size are noise. In an animal model with one record
per animal it is also attenuated, because the breeding value of an animal absorbs part of
the extra residual variance of its group: with h2 = 0.4 it averaged 0.112 for a true
0.173 and 0.222 for 0.35, while a sire model with forty records per group gave 0.168 and
0.331 (\code{poder}, 500 data sets each). With classes of fewer than five effective degrees of
freedom there is no effect (\code{NA}). A non-rejection with small groups does not prove
homoscedasticity: with twice the residual variance in half of contemporary groups of 2 to
40 records (640 records) the dispersion check rejected 0.34 of the time, and with groups
of two to four 0.046, no more than under the model (0.12 with a residual SD varying by a
factor of about 1.4). With groups that small the kurtosis of all the residuals absorbs
the heteroscedasticity and the tail gets matched; \code{brown_forsythe} had 0.10 and 0.35
there (\code{poder}, 500 data sets). Several checks are run at once: \code{p_adjusted} is Holm's
adjustment over the primary rows.

The p-value is conditional on the estimated components: a component at a boundary is held
there.

Records with \code{1 - h_ii < 1e-8} (a contemporary group of one record: the residual is zero
by construction) and classes with a single record are left out and counted in \verb{$notes}.

OUTLIERS (\verb{$outliers}): the \code{n_outliers} largest \verb{|r|}, with a Bonferroni p \verb{min(1, 2 n Phi(-|r|))}, the leverage and a generalized Cook distance \verb{r^2 h / ((1 - h) sum(h))}: the
change of all the solutions, fixed AND random, when the record is deleted with the
components held, in the metric of the mixed model equations (Hodges 1998), which in an
animal model is dominated by the record's own breeding value. It is not the Cook distance
of the fixed effects alone (Christensen, Pearson and Johnson 1992).

RANDOM EFFECTS (\verb{$random}): the standardized prediction \verb{z_j = u_j / sqrt(s2 K_jj - PEV_j)}, unit variance under the model (\verb{Var(u^) = G - C^uu}, Henderson 1975), with
\code{K_jj} the diagonal of the covariance matrix the mixed model equations of the fit used,
read from the selective inverse of its inverse when the design is rebuilt: \code{1 + F} for a
pedigree term, \code{H_ii} for every animal of a single step (genotyped or not; the prior
variance \code{\link[=accuracy]{accuracy()}} uses for a non-genotyped animal is \code{1 + F}, which is not its
variance under \code{H}), the diagonal of K for a \code{kernel()} term, and 1 for an iid term. A
level with a reliability \verb{1 - PEV / (s2 K_jj)} under \code{min_reliability} (an animal without
information) has no standardization and is left out and counted. Scalar terms only; a
reaction norm is skipped.

A \code{\link[=gibbs]{gibbs()}} chain is diagnosed by pivotal discrepancy measures (Johnson 2007; Yuan and
Johnson 2012): each stored draw of the realized residual \code{e_s / sqrt(s2e_s)} is iid
N(0, 1) under the model (a posterior draw of the realized errors, not an estimate), so
the same statistics have an exact reference distribution, simulated here from \code{nsim}
standard normal vectors, with no leverage and no confounding. One p-value per draw; the
reported one is \verb{min(1, 2 median)}, since the median of p-values that are each uniform is
at most \code{alpha} with probability at most \verb{2 alpha} (Markov), so it is a conservative
test. The argument needs proper priors and is approximate under the flat prior of \code{b}.
The posterior predictive p-value of Gelman, Meng and Stern (1996) is not used: it is not
uniform under the model and concentrates near 0.5. Measured
(\verb{validation/residual_diagnostics_calibration.R pivotal}, 200 simulated data sets per
cell, 20 draws, \code{nsim = 199}): under the model (contemporary groups of 2 to 40, 640
records) the mean fraction of draws with \code{p < 0.05} (column \code{draws_below_0.05}) was 0.044
to 0.055 for every check, and the combined p rejected 0.000 to 0.010 at 5\%; with a
residual SD varying by a factor of about 1.4 across groups (2000 records) the combined p
of \code{dispersion} rejected 1.000, of \code{normality} 0.72 (the mixture of variances is not
normal) and of \code{koenker} against the covariate 0.005. For \code{family = "probit"} the draws
are of the latent residual (variance 1): under the model the mean fraction of draws with
\code{p < 0.05} was 0.037 to 0.049 and the combined p rejected none of 200 data sets
(\code{pivotal_probit}, 640 records). The chain is checked against the data too: the design
rebuilt from \code{data} times the posterior means must give the fitted values the chain
stored, and each random group must have the levels of the chain. Beyond that the pedigree
and the genotypes are NOT checked against the chain (it keeps nothing of its \code{K} to
compare with): they enter only the \code{K_jj} of the standardized breeding values, so another
pedigree or other genotypes with the same animals change those \code{z} without an error, and
only those. The standardized breeding values of a chain also use the posterior means of
the components and the posterior variances in place of the PEV, and are approximate and
not calibrated.

COST. The design is built, and the mixed model equations and each \code{K^-1} factorized, once
per call; each replicate is then one draw and one solve with that factor. In a single
step the build (G, its inverse, H^-1 and the diagonal of H) is most of the cost: with 2000
genotyped animals among 3600 records it took 6.9 to 9.3 s, and the whole check with
\code{nsim = 39} 7.6 to 8.7 s, against 9.8 s for the fit at given components (\code{tempo_su}, one
thread, three runs on the same data). The replicates come in batches
of at most \code{1.25e7 / n} (two matrices of about 100 MB each), whose statistics are kept
and the replicates dropped, so memory does not grow with \code{nsim}. When the tail is matched
the work is \verb{2 nsim + 49} replicates instead of \code{nsim}. In an animal model of 24,000
records in 777 contemporary groups, one thread, the same fit and data three times, with
\code{nsim = 199} and normal replicates (the tail was not matched): 84, 94 and 100 s (\code{tempo};
the \code{\link[=model]{model()}} fit itself took 734 s), on a machine shared with other jobs.

Not available: \code{\link[=model_mt]{model_mt()}} (the residuals are; the bootstrap of the multi-trait fit is
not), a multi-trait \code{\link[=model_ar1]{model_ar1()}}, and the threshold and survival fits, whose residuals
are plug-in and not calibrated (see \code{\link[=residuals.breeding_fit]{residuals.breeding_fit()}}). The validation of the
genetic trend of an evaluation (Boichard et al. 1995, the basis of the Interbull trend
tests) and of its Mendelian sampling variance across years (Tyriseva et al. 2018) check an
evaluation over time, not the residuals of one fit, and are not here. Neither is the
score-based test of heterogeneity in linear mixed models of Wang et al. (2021), which was
not compared with these checks.
}
